---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import DevEncodeSubnet from '@/components/tools/dev/DevEncodeSubnet.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="subnet-calculator">
  <DevEncodeSubnet client:load mode="subnet" />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>What the subnet prefix does</h2>
    <p>
      An IPv4 address has 32 bits. The prefix says how many of those bits identify the network; the other
      bits identify a host in it. A <code>/24</code> fixes the first 24 bits, which leaves 8 host bits and so
      2⁸ = 256 addresses.
    </p>
    <pre><code>address 192.168.  1. 10  = 11000000 10101000 00000001 00001010
mask    255.255.255.  0  = 11111111 11111111 11111111 00000000
AND ---------------------------------------------------------
network 192.168.  1.  0  = 11000000 10101000 00000001 00000000</code></pre>
    <p>
      All other figures follow from that AND. The broadcast address is the network with all host bits set,
      the wildcard mask is the inverted mask, and the host range is everything between them.
    </p>

    <h2>Subnet reference table</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Prefix</th><th>Mask</th><th>Addresses</th><th>Usable hosts</th><th>Cloud usable</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>/16</td><td>255.255.0.0</td><td>65,536</td><td>65,534</td><td>65,531</td></tr>
          <tr><td>/20</td><td>255.255.240.0</td><td>4,096</td><td>4,094</td><td>4,091</td></tr>
          <tr><td>/24</td><td>255.255.255.0</td><td>256</td><td>254</td><td>251</td></tr>
          <tr><td>/25</td><td>255.255.255.128</td><td>128</td><td>126</td><td>123</td></tr>
          <tr><td>/26</td><td>255.255.255.192</td><td>64</td><td>62</td><td>59</td></tr>
          <tr><td>/27</td><td>255.255.255.224</td><td>32</td><td>30</td><td>27</td></tr>
          <tr><td>/28</td><td>255.255.255.240</td><td>16</td><td>14</td><td>11</td></tr>
          <tr><td>/30</td><td>255.255.255.252</td><td>4</td><td>2</td><td>Not allowed</td></tr>
          <tr><td>/31</td><td>255.255.255.254</td><td>2</td><td>2 (RFC 3021)</td><td>Not allowed</td></tr>
          <tr><td>/32</td><td>255.255.255.255</td><td>1</td><td>1 (single host)</td><td>Not allowed</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Why the cloud column is different</h2>
    <p>
      AWS, Azure and GCP each reserve five addresses in every subnet, not two. In an AWS VPC subnet
      <code>10.0.0.0/24</code>:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><code>10.0.0.0</code>: network address</li>
      <li><code>10.0.0.1</code>: the VPC router</li>
      <li><code>10.0.0.2</code>: DNS resolver</li>
      <li><code>10.0.0.3</code>: reserved for future use</li>
      <li><code>10.0.0.255</code>: broadcast address (reserved although VPCs do not support broadcast)</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      AWS also rejects subnets smaller than /28, which leaves eleven usable addresses after reservations.
      Turn on cloud mode above to see the correct count for your block.
    </p>

    <h2>Planning with VLSM</h2>
    <p>
      Variable-length subnet masking gives each subnet only the space it needs, in place of an even split.
      The method:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>List each subnet you need with its host count, largest first.</li>
      <li>Round each up to the next power of two. Add two for network and broadcast, or five in a cloud VPC.</li>
      <li>Allocate the largest from the start of the block, then the next, and so on.</li>
      <li>Leave the end of the block free for growth; expansion later is much easier than renumbering.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      The split table above lists every subnet at a chosen prefix, which you can use as the working document.
    </p>

    <h2>How IPv6 differs</h2>
    <p>
      IPv6 has no broadcast address and no host-count arithmetic, because every LAN gets a /64 however many
      devices it has; SLAAC requires this. Site allocations are usually /48, which holds 65,536 of those
      /64s. The IPv6 panel above uses BigInt, so a /32 shows its 79,228,162,514,264,337,593,543,950,336
      addresses exactly, not as a rounded float.
    </p>

    <h2>No lookups</h2>
    <p>
      The subnet calculator does arithmetic on the bits you typed. There is no WHOIS, geolocation or reverse
      DNS, because each needs a network call and this site makes none. The tool works with the network
      disconnected, for example on a plane.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
